{
  // WHAT A PUBLISHED PACKAGE EMITS, in one place because four packages already forgot it
  // (rockysurf-sxbm).
  //
  // `tsconfig.base.json` turns both maps on, which is right for local development: every
  // workspace package has its `src/` next to its `dist/`, so go-to-definition works. It is wrong
  // for the PUBLISHED artifact. Every package here ships `dist` and not `src`, so each map's
  // `sources: ["../src/*.ts"]` dangles in a consumer's `node_modules` — a path that does not
  // exist on their disk.
  //
  // That is not cosmetic. The dogfood run behind rockysurf-92nv had every `ConformanceError`
  // stack pointing at `provider-conformance/src/index.ts`, which sent an out-of-tree author
  // looking for a file nobody had shipped.
  //
  // MEASURED at the commit that added this file, with `npm pack --dry-run --json`: the maps were
  // 28-31% of unpacked size in the four provider packages, and 3,525,083 bytes across 163 files
  // in `@rockysurf/core` — 55% of it. Core is what `npx rockysurf` installs, so that share was
  // being paid on every cold start. Re-measure rather than quoting these; they are a snapshot.
  //
  // The alternatives were considered and are worse. Shipping `src/` alongside makes the maps
  // resolve but the tarball larger, and puts sources in consumers' `node_modules` that nobody
  // reads. `inlineSources` embeds the source IN the maps, which is larger still. Doc comments
  // survive either way: tsc copies them into the `.d.ts`, which is what an editor shows on hover,
  // and anyone actually debugging a provider is doing it in this monorepo where `src/` is right
  // there.
  //
  // `scripts/verify-tarballs.mjs` fails if any published tarball contains a `.map`, so this is a
  // gate rather than a convention four packages can quietly drift from again.
  "compilerOptions": {
    "declarationMap": false,
    "sourceMap": false
  }
}
